Yes, you can determine whether a Slack message has been read or not using the Slack API. To achieve this, you need to use the `message.read` event from the Slack Events API. This event is triggered whenever a user reads a message in a channel or direct message.

Here's an example of how you can handle the `message.read` event in your Slack app:

1. First, you need to subscribe to the `message.read` event using the Slack Events API. You can do this by adding the following code to your app's configuration file (e.g., `slack_events.json`):
```json
{
  "events": [
    {
      "type": "message.read",
      "event": "message.read"
    }
  ]
}
```
1. Next, you need to handle the `message.read` event in your app's code. You can do this by adding the following code to your app's handler function (e.g., `handle_event.js`):
```javascript
const { Message } = require('slack');

function handleMessageRead(event) {
  const message = new Message(event.message);
  const channel = message.channel;
  const user = message.user;

  // Check if the message has been read
  if (message.read) {
    console.log(`Message ${message.id} in channel ${channel} read by ${user}`);
  } else {
    console.log(`Message ${message.id} in channel ${channel} unread by ${user}`);
  }
}

module.exports = handleMessageRead;
```
1. Finally, you need to call the `handleMessageRead` function whenever a `message.read` event is received. You can do this by adding the following code to your app's main file (e.g., `app.js`):
```javascript
const { handleMessageRead } = require('./handle_event');

app.on('message.read', handleMessageRead);
```
By following these steps, you can determine whether a Slack message has been read or not using the Slack API.

