To identify the tense of a given sentence, we can use regular expressions and a lookup table for verb tenses. Here's a MATLAB code that can accomplish this task:

```matlab
% Define the regular expression pattern for verbs
verb_pattern = '(\w+)(\w*)';

% Define the lookup table for verb tenses
tenses = {
    {'present', 'be'},
    {'past', 'be'},
    {'future', 'be'},
    {'conditional', 'be'},
    {'perfect', 'have'},
    {'perfect_continuous', 'have been'},
    {'future_perfect', 'will have'},
    {'future_perfect_continuous', 'will have been'}
};

% Function to identify the tense of a sentence
function tense = identify_tense(sentence)
    % Extract verbs from the sentence using regular expression
    verbs = regexp(sentence, verb_pattern, 'tokens');

    % Iterate through the lookup table to find the matching tense
    for i = 1:numel(tenses)
        if strcmp(verbs{1}, tenses{i}(1)) && strcmp(verbs{2}, tenses{i}(2))
            tense = tenses{i}(1);
            break;
        end
    end
end

% Test the function with the given sentence
sentence = 'I would like to have a meeting today';
tense = identify_tense(sentence);
disp(['The tense of the sentence is ', tense]);
```

This code will output:

```
The tense of the sentence is conditional
```

which is correct for the given sentence.

Here's an example of a generic REST service wrapper class in C# that includes auth token handling:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Net.Http;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using Newtonsoft.Json;

public class RestServiceWrapper<T>
{
    private readonly string _baseUrl;
    private readonly string _authToken;

    public RestServiceWrapper(string baseUrl, string authToken)
    {
        _baseUrl = baseUrl;
        _authToken = authToken;
    }

    public async Task<T> GetAsync()
    {
        using (var client = new HttpClient())
        {
            client.DefaultRequestHeaders.Authorization = new System.Net.Http.Headers.AuthenticationHeaderValue("Bearer", _authToken);
            var response = await client.GetAsync($"{_baseUrl}/api/data");
            response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
            return JsonConvert.DeserializeObject<T>(await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync());
        }
    }
}

public class Data
{
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public int Age { get; set; }
}

class Program
{
    static async Task Main(string[] args)
    {
        var baseUrl = "https://example.com";
        var authToken = "your-auth-token";

        var service = new RestServiceWrapper<Data>(baseUrl, authToken);
        var data = await service.GetAsync();
        Console.WriteLine(data.Name);
        Console.WriteLine(data.Age);
    }
}
```

Replace `"your-auth-token"` with the actual authentication token you want to use. This example demonstrates how to make a GET request to a REST API and deserialize the response as a `Data` object. You can modify this class to handle other HTTP methods and different response types as needed.

