To achieve this, you can use Google Apps Script along with the Google Maps API. Here's a simple script that searches for a place name in a column and stores the latitude and longitude in other columns:

```javascript
function searchPlace() {
  var sheet = SpreadsheetApp.getActiveSpreadsheet().getActiveSheet();
  var dataRange = sheet.getRange("A2:A"); // Assuming your data starts in cell A2
  var placeName = dataRange.getValue();

  var geocoder = new google.maps.Geocoder();
  geocoder.geocode({ 'address': placeName }, function(results, status) {
    if (status == google.maps.GeocoderStatus.OK) {
      var latitude = results[0].geometry.location.lat();
      var longitude = results[0].geometry.location.lng();

      sheet.getRange("B2").setValue(latitude); // Store latitude in column B
      sheet.getRange("C2").setValue(longitude); // Store longitude in column C
    } else {
      sheet.getRange("B2").setValue("Error"); // Set an error value if geocoding fails
      sheet.getRange("C2").setValue("Error"); // Set an error value if geocoding fails
    }
  });
}
```

This script assumes that your data is located in column A, starting from row 2. You can adjust the `dataRange` variable to match your actual data setup.

