To resolve this issue, you can try setting the 'Access-Control-Allow-Origin' header to a specific domain or list of domains that are allowed to access your API. This will ensure that only requests from authorized sources can include an authorization header.

Here's an example of how you can set the 'Access-Control-Allow-Origin' header in Node.js using the `express` framework:
```javascript
const express = require('express');
const app = express();

app.use((req, res, next) => {
  res.header('Access-Control-Allow-Origin', 'https://example.com');
  next();
});

// Your API routes go here

app.listen(3000);
```
In this example, the 'Access-Control-Allow-Origin' header is set to 'https://example.com', which means that only requests from this domain will be allowed to include an authorization header. You can replace 'https://example.com' with any other domain or list of domains that you want to allow.

