Here's a simple decorator for Nest.js middleware that checks the `NODE_ENV` variable and returns `false` if it's not set to `true`. If `NODE_ENV` is set to `true`, it calls the next function in the middleware chain.

```typescript
import { Injectable, CanActivate } from '@nestjs/common';

@Injectable()
export class EnvironmentGuard implements CanActivate {
  canActivate(context: any): boolean {
    const nodeEnv = process.env.NODE_ENV;
    return nodeEnv === 'true';
  }
}
```

To use this guard, you can create a middleware function that uses it:

```typescript
import { Injectable, MiddlewareFunction } from '@nestjs/common';
import { EnvironmentGuard } from './environment.guard';

@Injectable()
export class MyMiddleware implements MiddlewareFunction {
  use(req: any, res: any, next: () => void) {
    const guard = new EnvironmentGuard();
    if (guard.canActivate(req)) {
      next();
    } else {
      res.status(403).send('Forbidden');
    }
  }
}
```

Then, you can add this middleware function to your route or controller:

```typescript
import { Controller, Get, UseMiddleware } from '@nestjs/common';
import { MyMiddleware } from './middleware/my.middleware';

@Controller('/')
export class MyController {
  @Get()
  @UseMiddleware(MyMiddleware)
  getHello(): string {
    return 'Hello World!';
  }
}
```

Now, when you access this route, the middleware function will check the `NODE_ENV` variable and return `false` if it's not set to `true`. If `NODE_ENV` is set to `true`, it will call the next function in the middleware chain and return the response.

