To update your application without replacing the existing executable, you can follow these steps:

1. Create a new folder in the same directory as the current executable, e.g., "update".
2. Extract the contents of the ZIP file into the "update" folder.
3. Modify the application's configuration file (e.g., app.config or appsettings.json) to point to the updated files in the "update" folder.
4. Restart the application.

Here's a more detailed explanation:

1. Create a new folder in the same directory as the current executable:

```csharp
string updateFolderPath = Path.Combine(Environment.CurrentDirectory, "update");
if (!Directory.Exists(updateFolderPath))
{
    Directory.CreateDirectory(updateFolderPath);
}
```

2. Extract the contents of the ZIP file into the "update" folder:

```csharp
using (ZipArchive zipArchive = ZipFile.OpenRead("path/to/your/zip/file.zip"))
{
    foreach (ZipArchiveEntry entry in zipArchive.Entries)
    {
        string entryPath = Path.Combine(updateFolderPath, entry.Name);
        entry.ExtractToFile(entryPath);
    }
}
```

3. Modify the application's configuration file to point to the updated files in the "update" folder:

For example, if your application's configuration file is named "app.config" and it contains a setting like this:

```xml
<add key="DataPath" value="C:\MyApplication\Data" />
```

You can modify it to point to the updated files in the "update" folder like this:

```xml
<add key="DataPath" value="C:\MyApplication\update\Data" />
```

4. Restart the application:

After modifying the configuration file, restart the application to apply the changes.

This approach allows you to update your application without replacing the existing executable, ensuring a smooth upgrade process for your users.

